<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glyndyfrdwy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Glyndyfrdwy is a village and former lordship in the Dee valley of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, between Corwen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangollen">Llangollen</a>. It was held by descendants of the princes of northern Powys, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a>, who took his name from it. There on 16 September 1400 his followers proclaimed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, beginning the last great Welsh revolt against English rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glyndyfrdwy.jpg" alt="Glyndyfrdwy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tooplanx · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glyndyfrdwy_village.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Glyndyfrdwy, sometimes written Glyn Dyfrdwy, lies on the A5 road halfway between Corwen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llangollen">Llangollen</a> in the valley of the river Dee, which is Afon Dyfrdwy in Welsh. In the 12th century a Norman castle motte was raised near the village to command the route through the valley. Known locally as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a>'s Mount, probably from mwnt, the Welsh word for a motte, it survives only as an eroded mound.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a>, Baron of Glyndyfrdwy, was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> at his manor here, beginning a rebellion against English rule that lasted 15 years. His manor hall was probably a square building defended by a water-filled moat, a palisade and a gate. In 1403 the site was devastated by the forces of Henry of Monmouth, the English Prince of Wales and later King Henry V.</p><p>The parish of Glyndyfrdwy was created in 1866 from the former Corwen townships of Carrog, Mwstwr and Tir Llanerch and parts of Bonwm and Rhagat. It lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merionethshire">Merionethshire</a> until 1974, when it passed to Clwyd under the Local Government Act 1972, and in 1996 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a>. The same year, 1866, a station opened on the Great Western Railway line from Ruabon through Corwen and Dolgellau to Barmouth. The village was also the terminus of the Deeside Tramway, a narrow gauge line built for the local slate quarries and one of the last industrial railways in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to use wooden rails.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Owain Glyndŵr Memorial Hall holds artefacts associated with Glyndŵr, among them a copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennal-letter">Pennal Letter</a> of 1405 to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a>, pictures of his Parliament house at Dolgellau and a replica of his Great Seal. The railway closed in the 1960s, but the heritage Llangollen Railway, founded by enthusiasts in 1977, ran its first passenger train to Glyndyfrdwy on 17 April 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Glyndyfrdwy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through the line of Madog ap Maredudd, the princes of northern Powys also gave rise to the lords of <b>Glyndyfrdwy</b>, from whom Owain Glyndŵr, leader of the Welsh revolt of 1400, descended.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Supporters at <b>Glyndyfrdwy</b> proclaimed Owain Glyndŵr Prince of Wales, launching a revolt against English rule.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-16">16 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
